Id: logf012w
Severity: WARN
Category: logging
Check: check_logfiles
Fix: Start the syslog daemon (systemctl enable --now rsyslog), or make the journal persistent.
References: rsyslog.conf(5); syslog-ng.conf(5); journald.conf(5); CIS Distribution Independent Linux Benchmark 4.2 (Configure Logging)
Controls: CIS 8.2, 8.3; NIST AU-9; ISO 8.15

A syslog daemon is configured on this Linux host (rsyslog by
/etc/rsyslog.conf, syslog-ng by its syslog-ng.conf, the classic syslogd
by /etc/syslog.conf), none is running (rsyslogd, syslog-ng, syslogd,
metalog), and the journal is not kept on disk (/var/log/journal is
missing or empty). Nothing records the system log: the files the daemon
wrote stop where it stopped, and look complete to whoever reads them
later, while whatever journald holds in memory goes with the next
reboot. A stopped or crashed rsyslog on a host whose journal is
volatile is exactly the quiet an intruder wants.

This is judged live only. Where journald runs with a volatile journal
and no syslog daemon, check_audit's logf008w says so as well; this
finding adds the configured daemon that is not doing its job. A host
whose journal is persistent keeps its logs whether rsyslog runs or not,
and gets nothing here.

Start the daemon ('systemctl enable --now rsyslog'), or, if the text
logs are not wanted, remove its configuration and make the journal
persistent (Storage=persistent in /etc/systemd/journald.conf, or
'mkdir /var/log/journal', then restart systemd-journald).
